WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
Many review failures come from trusting shape, type, ownership, or caller intent too early.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:19.080
Node.js services receive data through req.body, req.query, req.params, headers, cookies, uploaded files, webhooks, queues, and third-party callbacks.

3
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:25.900
AI can help compare accepted input against schemas, controller expectations, and business rules.

4
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:37.600
The reviewer should confirm that validation happens before business logic and that validation includes type, size, range, allowed values, and required relationships where relevant.

5
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.520
Input review should connect directly to data access.

6
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:51.580
Ask the assistant to trace how untrusted values reach database queries, ORM calls, search filters, file paths, message publishing, or outbound requests.

7
00:00:51.680 --> 00:01:03.920
Review for unsafe object merging, mass assignment, excessive update fields, missing tenant conditions, parameterization mistakes, type coercion surprises, and file handling gaps.

8
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:12.100
AI may identify a suspicious flow, but the reviewer should validate it against actual code paths, framework behavior, and tests.

9
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.820
Output safety matters as much as input safety.

10
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:27.120
A handler can validate input correctly and still return too much data, expose internal fields, leak stack traces, include secret values, or shape errors inconsistently.

11
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:35.540
Ask AI to compare response objects against what the client actually needs and to identify raw database objects or debug details returned directly.

12
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.720
Secure review should confirm both sides of the flow: what the application accepts and what it sends back.
